## 1. Introduction
With the rapid development and use of various electronic equipment and wireless communication equipment, problems such as electromagnetic radiation pollution will inevitably arise, posing a serious threat to military applications and human health [1,2]. As we all know, the development of electromagnetic wave absorbing materials plays a vital role in solving these problems. The ideal electromagnetic wave absorbing material should have the characteristics of light weight, strong absorption capacity, thin thickness and wide frequency [3]. However, absorbing materials with a single dielectric loss or magnetic loss mechanism have limited attenuation capabilities, and it is difficult to meet all the above requirements. Therefore, combining two or more materials with a synergistic absorption effect of dielectric loss and magnetic loss is widely considered to be an effective way to construct a new type of absorbing material [4–6].
$\mathtt{B@15.3}$ GHz with a thickness of $1.6\ \mathrm{mm}$ . Therefore, designing and constructing special structured multi-component MOFsbased carbonized materials is an effective method for preparing lightweight and high-efficiency electromagnetic wave absorbing materials.
In this work, bimetallic MOFs with core–shell structure (ZIF67@ZIF8) are synthesized by a one-step method. Then using these as the structural template, ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}.6{\sf H}_{2}0$ as the etchant, $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ nanocages are prepared by Ni doping and vacuum carbonization. By changing the etching time, the structure and composition of $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ can be indirectly and effectively adjusted, thereby greatly improving the electromagnetic wave absorption performance. The hollow structure can not only match the dielectric constant and impedance, but also realize multiple scattering, multi interface polarization and dipole polarization. We believe that the strategy of introducing Ni particles into the binary MOFs provides a new idea for optimizing the magnetic absorbing materials based on MOFs, which is very important for the development of new absorbing agents.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
2-Methylimidazole was purchased from Shanghai Titan Scientific Co. Ltd. Methanol and Ethanol were provided from Guangdong Guanghua Sci-Tech Co. Ltd. $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and Ni $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ were bought from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. All chemical reagents are analytically pure and need no further processing.
## 2.2. Preparation of ZIF67@ZIF8
Typically, $2.184\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ $2.233\ \mathrm{g}$ of $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $2.364\:\mathrm{g}$ of 2-Methylimidazole were dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol, $30~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol and $60~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol, respectively, to obtain solution A, solution B and solution C. Subsequently, solution A was poured into solution C and stirred at room temperature for $5\ \mathrm{min}$ , and then solution B was added to the above-mentioned mixed solution of $A+C,$ and the reaction was continued for $25{\mathrm{~min}}$ . After the reaction, the purple-pink powder collected by centrifugation was ZIF67@ZIF8, then thoroughly washed with methanol, and dried overnight in a vacuum oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$
## 2.3. Preparation of Core-Shell ZIF67@ZIF8/Ni2+
First, added $0.2{\mathrm{g}}$ of ZIF67 $@$ ZIF8 to $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol, sonicated it until it was completely dispersed. After that, quickly injected it into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol solution containing $_{0.45\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , and stirred at room temperature for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ , $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ and $90~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The respective products were collected by centrifugation and named $\mathrm{ZIF67@ZIF8/Ni^{2+}}{-30},$ $\mathrm{ZIF67@ZIF8/Ni^{2+}{-}}60$ and $Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{IF67@ZIF8/\Omega}$ $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ -90, and finally dried overnight in a vacuum oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf C$
## 2.4. Preparation of CoZn/NC and Co@ZnO/Ni@NC
## 2.5. Characterization
The morphology and structure of the composite materials were observed by Scanning electron microscope (SEM, FEI Verios G4) and transmission electron microscope (TEM, FEI TALOS-F200X). Using X-ray diffraction (XRD, Thermo Scientific 7000) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscope (XPS, Kratos Axis Ultra DLD) to analyze the crystal structure and surface components. The Raman spectra were collected by laser confocal Raman microspectroscopy (WITec Alpha300R) with a laser excitation of $532~\mathrm{nm}$ . The magnetic properties were recorded by a physical property measurement system (PPMS, CFMS-14 t). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA, Mettler Toledo Q50) was carried out under a $0_{2}$ atmosphere in the temperature range of $35\sim700^{\circ}C$ at a heating rate of $10~\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ . The relative complex permittivity and the relative complex permeability were measured by vector network analyzer (Anritsu MS463222B) using coaxial line method.
## 3. Results and discussion
The preparation process of the $\mathsf{C o@Z n O/N i@N C}$ nanocage was shown in Fig. 1. First, $C o^{2+}$ in methanol was coordinated with dimethylimidazole to form a ZIF67 polyhedron. After that, the obtained ZIF67 served as the seed for the epitaxial growth of
ZIF8, and the ZIF67 $@$ ZIF8 polyhedron was obtained. Dispersed the prepared ZIF67@ZIF8 in an ethanol solution containing $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , and stirred to obtain a core–shell structure of $\mathrm{ZIF67@ZIF8/Ni^{2+}}$ . Finally, it was carbonized in vacuum to obtain a hollow $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ nanocage.
The morphology and structure of ZIF67@ZIF8, ZIF67@ZIF8 $/\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and Co@ZnO/Ni@NC were characterized by SEM and TEM. As shown in Fig. 2A, the ZIF67@ZIF8 particles had the shape of a rhombus dodecahedron, the surface was relatively smooth, and the size was in the range of $800{-}900~\mathrm{nm}$ . The XRD pattern (Figure S1) showed a strong typical diffraction peak of ZIF67@ZIF8, indicating its good crystallinity and purity [20,21]. The TEM image (Fig. 2B) further confirmed the formation of rhombic dodecahedron particles. In order to clearly distinguish the formation of the core– shell structure of ZIF67@ZIF8 and the interface between the ZIF67 core and the ZIF8 shell, elemental mapping analysis was carried out. The corresponding element maps showed the distribution of C, N, O, Co and Zn respectively (Fig. 2C). The Co element can be clearly detected in the core area, while the Zn element was mainly distributed in the shell area, which confirmed the successful formation of the core–shell structure ZIF67@ZIF8.
Fig. 3A, B and C showed that compared with ZIF67 $@$ ZIF8, the surface of $\mathrm{ZIF67@ZIF8/Ni^{2+}}$ was relatively rough. This was because in the process of the reaction, the protons produced by the hydrolysis of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ gradually corroded the ZIF8 shell from the outside, and the released $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ may be oxidized by the dissolved $0_{2}$ to form $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ At high temperature, $C0^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ were reduced to magnetic Co and Ni. A ZnO/Ni shell and Co core were formed. It can be seen from Fig. 3D, E and F that with the increase of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ etching time, the $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}/N i}}$ shell became obvious, the interior gradually became hollow, and the volume of the cavity became larger and larger, which was also confirmed from the broken SEM image (Figure S2A). In Fig. 3G, H and I, it can be observed that after vacuum carbonization, the size of the polyhedron was significantly reduced due to the inward shrinkage caused by the burning of organic components at $600^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . In addition, the loss of organic ligands after pyrolysis caused the core–shell structure of $\mathrm{ZIF67@ZIF8/Ni^{2+}}$ to evolve into porous hollow structure $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ nanocages, and we can clearly see the $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ nanocage had an inner cavity from Figure S2B. The TEM images (Fig. 3J, K, and L) further characterized the nanocage with a tightly packed structure. The corresponding element mapping of $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC-60}$ confirmed that the Co element was mainly distributed in the inner shell of the nanocage. The Zn and Ni elements were mainly distributed in the outer shell of the nanocage (Fig. 3M). The content of each metal element in $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC-30,~Co@ZnO/Ni@NC-60}$ and $\mathtt{C o@Z n O/}$ ${\mathsf{N i@N C-90}}$ was also given in Figure S3.
As shown in Figure 4, in order to confirm its structure in more detail, HRTEM analysis was carried out on Co@ZnO/Ni@NC-60. The application research demonstrated that the sample had the best absorbing performance. As can be seen from Figure 4B, a small amount of carbon nanotubes were grown on the surface of $\mathsf{C o@Z n O/N i@N C},$ the length was about $30{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ . The Selected Area Electron Diffraction image (SAED) was also used to prove the crystal structure of $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ (Fig. 4C). Each diffraction ring corresponded to the (111), (200), and (220) planes of Co particles and the (100) and (102) planes of $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ respectively [22,23]. The ordered lattice fringes can be clearly identified from HRTEM images. The gaps between the two adjacent lattice fringes were about $0.34\mathrm{nm}$ , $0.25~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $0.21~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which were well distributed to the (00 2) crystal plane of graphite carbon, the (101) crystal plane of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and the (1 11) crystal plane of Co particles. At the same time, the coverage of the porous carbon matrix can be clearly observed around these heterogeneous nanoparticles (Fig. 4D and E) [24–26].
The crystal structure of $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ was characterized by XRD analysis. The result was shown in Fig. 5A, the protruding peaks at $44.20^{\circ}$ , $51.2^{\circ}$ and $75.9^{\circ}$ corresponded to the (111), (200) and (220) crystal faces of ${\sf C}_{0}$ particles, while the characteristic peaks at $44.5^{\circ}$ and $51.8^{\circ}$ corresponded to the (111) and (200) crystal faces of Ni particles [7,27]. In addition, all samples showed strong typical diffraction peaks of $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ (JCPDS: 36–1451), however, the peak of carbon was not obvious. This was because at the pyrolysis temperature of $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , the degree of graphitization of carbon was low. Therefore, the two typical characteristic peaks of carbon were investigated through the Raman spectrum (Fig. 5B). The characteristic peaks of all samples in the D band near $1350~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and the G band near $1585~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were the characteristics of disordered carbon and graphitic carbon, respectively. Therefore, $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ values were often used to evaluate the degree of graphitization of various carbon materials. The higher the value, the greater the degree of graphitization [28,29]. Obviously, $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC-90}$ had the highest $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value, which was mainly due to the catalytic effect of the transition metal Ni. In Fig. 5C, the TGA curve showed that compared with $\mathsf{C o Z n/N C}$ , $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ had a slow rise process at $200{-}300\ \mathrm{~\textdegree}$ , which was mainly due to the oxidation of Ni in the $\mathsf{C o@Z n O/N i@N C}$ shell. In addition, it was not difficult to find the thermal stability of $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ was higher than that of ${\mathsf{C o Z n/N C}}.$ This was because the ZnO-Ni shell acted as a surface stabilizing substance, and with the increased of the $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ etching time, the residue also increased. Due to the presence of magnetic Co and Ni nanoparticles, the saturation magnetization (Ms) of ${\mathsf{C o Z n/N C}}$ was $21.2\mathrm{\emu/g}$ . As the content of Ni particles increased, the $\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{S}}$ values of $\mathsf{C o@Z n O/N i@N C-30}$ , $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}.$ 60 and $\mathtt{C o@Z n O/}$ ${\mathsf{N i@N C-90}}$ reached 25.8, 30.1 and $46.8\mathrm{\emu/g}$ , respectively. Meanwhile, the coercivity $\left(\operatorname{H}_{\mathsf{C}}\right)$ of $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ also increased with the increase of Ni particle content. Higher $\mathsf{M}_{\mathsf{S}}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{C}}$ values helped high-frequency resonance and enhance the electromagnetic wave absorption performance in the high-frequency field (Fig. 5D).
XPS spectroscopy was used to analyse the elemental composition of $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC{-}}60$ (Fig. 6). Fig. 6A was the full spectrum of $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC{-}}60$ , it was clear that $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC{-}60}$ contained six elements: C, N, O, Co, Zn and Ni. The peaks in the C 1 s spectrum at 288.1, 285.4, and 284.3 eV (Fig. 6B) were attributed to CAO, CAN, and $c{-}c$ , respectively. In the N 1 s spectrum (Fig. 6C), pyridine N, pyrrole N and graphite N with binding energies of 398.0 eV, 399.9 eV and $400.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ were included. The $Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}\ 2\boldsymbol{\mathrm{p}}$ spectrum had two strong peaks at $1019.4~\mathrm{eV}$ and $1042.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 6D), which were attributed to $Z\mathrm{n}~2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}~2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ . The spectrum of $\mathtt{C o2p}$ showed two main peaks at $795.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $779.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ respectively, and they were assigned to $\mathtt{C o\ 2p}_{1/2}$ and Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , respectively. The Co metal state and $C0^{2+}$ oxidation state of the Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ peak were $778.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $781.0\ \mathrm{eV}.$ , and the peak at $800.4{\mathrm{~eV}}$ was attributed to the satellite peak (Fig. 6E). In Fig. 6F, Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ and Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{2/3}$ peaks were observed at 872 eV and $853~\mathrm{eV}$ . The spectrum of Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ showed two peaks at $852.9\mathrm{eV}$ and $854.1\mathrm{eV}$ , which were the metallic state of $\mathsf{N i}$ and the oxidation state of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ . In addition, two satellite peaks were found at 860 and $878{\mathrm{~eV}}$ [30–32].
The absorbing performance of electromagnetic wave absorbing materials was evaluated by the reflection loss (RL) value, and it was calculated by the following formula [33,34]:
$$
\mathsf{Z}=\left|\frac{\mathsf{Z}_{\mathrm{in}}}{\mathsf{Z}_{0}}\right|=\sqrt{\left|\mu_{r}/\varepsilon_{r}\right|}\operatorname{tanh}\left[j(2\pi f d/c)\sqrt{\mu_{r}\varepsilon_{r}}\right]
$$
$$
\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{L}}(\mathrm{d}\mathrm{B})=20{\log_{10}}{\left|{\frac{\mathrm{Z}_{\mathrm{in}}-\mathrm{Z}_{0}}{\mathrm{Z}_{\mathrm{in}}+\mathrm{Z}_{0}}}\right|}
$$
Where $Z_{\mathrm{in}}$ was the input impedance of the absorber, $Z_{0}$ was the impedance of free space, $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{\Gamma}=\varepsilon^{\prime}-j\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}}$ was the relative complex permittivity, $\mu_{\mathrm{r}}{=}\mu^{\prime}{-}j\mu^{\prime\prime}$ was the relative complex permeability, $f$ was the frequency of the microwave, $d$ was the thickness of the absorber and $c$ was the speed of light.
It was not difficult to find that the ${\mathrm{RL}}_{\operatorname*{min}}$ value of $\mathtt{C o@Z n O/}$ ${\mathsf{N i@N C-60}}$ gradually shifted to low frequency as the thickness increased (Fig. 8). This can be explained by the quarterwavelength theory [36–38]:
$$
t_{m}={\frac{n{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}}{4}}={\frac{n c}{4f_{m}{\sqrt{|\mu_{r}||\varepsilon_{r}|}}}}(n=1,3,5\cdots)
$$
The electromagnetic wave attenuation ability was closely related to dielectric loss and magnetic loss. Dielectric loss usually included conductivity loss and polarization loss, which mainly included ion polarization, electronic polarization, dipolar polarization and interface polarization [41]. In the range of $2{\mathrm{-}}18{\mathrm{~GHz}}$ , all ion polarization and electronic polarization can be eliminated. Therefore, the polarization mechanism should be attributed to dipolarization and interface polarization. According to free electron theory and Debye theory, $\mathbf{\delta}\mathbf{\varepsilon}^{\prime}$ and $\bar{\mathbf{\varepsilon}}^{\prime\prime}$ can be expressed as [42,43]
$$
\varepsilon^{'}=\varepsilon_{\infty}+\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{1+\left(2\pi f\right)^{2}\tau^{2}}
$$
$$
\varepsilon^{"}=\varepsilon_{p}^{"}+\varepsilon_{c}^{"}=(\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty})\frac{2\pi f\tau}{1+\left(2\pi f\right)^{2}}+\frac{\sigma}{2\pi f\varepsilon_{0}}
$$
$$
\left(\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}^{\prime}-\frac{\varepsilon_{s}+\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}+\left(\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}^{\prime\prime}\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}
$$
It can be seen from the equation that the relationship curve between $\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ was a semicircle, and it was defined as a ColeCole semicircle. Each Cole-Cole semicircle corresponded to a Debye relaxation process. From Fig. 10, we can see several obvious semicircles on the curves of $\mathsf{C o@Z n O/N i@N C-30}$ and $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC-}$ 60, indicating that the Debye relaxation process had occurred. At the same time, a twisted semicircle was also observed in $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC-90}$ , which was caused by excessive Ni and large cavity structure. As we all know, multiple relaxation processes can be attributed to the special structure of the material. The presence of surface-grown carbon nanotubes and Co, Ni, and ZnO nanoparticles will create more interfaces, resulting in more interface polarization. The hollow structure greatly increased the specific surface area, causing more dipole polarization sites, which was conducive to more dipole polarization. The formation of CoNi and the increase in the degree of graphitization contributed to the increase of electrical conductivity, and it will lead to an increase in electrical conductivity loss. Magnetic loss mainly depended on natural resonance, exchange resonance and eddy current loss. If the magnetic loss was only caused by eddy current loss, when the frequency changed, the value of $C_{0}\left(\mathsf C_{0}=\mathsf\mu^{\prime\prime}\left(\mathsf\mu^{\prime}\right)^{-2}f^{-1}\right)$ should remain constant [44]. As shown in Fig. 10D, the ${\sf C}_{0}$ value of the $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ nanocage fluctuated greatly in the low-frequency region $(2{-}12~\mathrm{GHz})$ , but remained constant in the high-frequency region $(12-18~\mathrm{GHz})\$ . It showed that the magnetic loss was caused by the natural resonance in the low frequency region and the eddy current loss in the high frequency region.
Based on dielectric loss and magnetic loss, the attenuation constant $({\mathfrak{a}})$ was introduced to evaluate the microwave attenuation capability of $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ . Generally, a higher value of $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ corresponded to a higher microwave attenuation capability. According to equation [45,46]
$$
\begin{array}{l}{\displaystyle\alpha=\frac{\sqrt{2}\pi f}{c}}\\ {\displaystyle\qquad\times\sqrt{\left(\mu\prime\prime\varepsilon\prime-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)+\sqrt{\left(\mu\prime\prime\varepsilon\prime-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}+\left(\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon\prime\prime+\mu\prime\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}}}}\end{array}
$$
From Fig. 11, except in the range of $11.6~\mathrm{GHz}.13.6~\mathrm{GHz}$ , the $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ value of $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC{-}}60$ was higher than that of $\mathtt{C o@Z n O/}$ ${\mathsf{N i@N C-30}}$ and $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC-90}$ . This meant that it can strongly attenuate or dissipate electromagnetic waves, and is consistent with its best absorbing performance.
Based on the above discussion, the microwave absorption mechanism of $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ was further studied systematically, as shown in Fig. 12. The excellent absorbing performance of $\mathsf{C o@Z n O/N i@N C}$ was mainly related to its special hollow structure, dielectric loss and magnetic loss. In the $\mathrm{Co@ZnO/Ni@NC}$ nanocage, Co, Ni and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanoparticles acted as nodes, the $N$ -doped graphitic carbon layer acted as a connector, which was conducive to the construction of an interconnected electron transport network, resulting in increased conduction loss. In the nanocage, multiple heterogeneous interfaces between the hollow cavity, Co core, $Z_{\mathrm{{nO-Ni}}}$ shell, and a small number of carbon nanotubes grown on the surface and the gaps between them will accumulate a large amount of unevenly distributed space charges, which will cause electromagnetic waves dissipated by interface polarization and dipolar polarization. The high impedance matching between the carbon nanotubes on the $\mathsf{C o@Z n O/N i@N C}$ surface and the Co, Ni, and $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ nanoparticles can make more incident electromagnetic wave be absorbed inside the material. In addition, under the influence of the alternating electromagnetic field, the magnetic CoNi particles themselves will increase the magnetic loss by causing natural resonance and eddy current loss, thereby hindering the transmission of electromagnetic waves. Fig. 13 compared the absorbing properties of various absorbing agents derived from MOFs, which showed that the synthesized $\mathsf{C o@Z n O/N i@N C}$ has outstanding advantages in low frequency bands, and can be used as an ideal high-efficiency absorbing material.
